# Code owners. GitHub requests a review from these owners on every pull request
# that touches a matching path.
#
# This repository has been public since 2026-09-08 (ADR-0006), so "pull requests
# from strangers" is a description of reality rather than an aspiration. The
# comment here used to say "assume pull requests from strangers" as a hedge; it
# is now the operating condition.
#
# .github/workflows contains a job with `pages: write` and `id-token: write`. A
# pull request that edits it is a pull request that can publish to the live site
# or mint an OIDC token, so it does not merge without the repository owner
# looking at it. What stops a fork pull request from *running* that job is a
# separate and stronger control - the invariant in docs/runbook.md 3, "a fork
# pull request cannot reach the deploy job". Read that before changing any
# workflow's `on:` or `permissions:` block. This file is the review control; the
# invariant is the execution control. They are not substitutes.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE DOES, AND THE ONE CASE WHERE IT DOES NOT.
# On a pull request these rules are ENFORCED, not advisory: `main` has classic
# branch protection with `require_code_owner_reviews: true` and one required
# approval, set 2026-09-08. A pull request touching a path below does not merge
# until its owner approves it, and pushing a new commit after an approval
# dismisses that approval.
#
# The exception, deliberately chosen: `enforce_admins: false`. Branch protection
# binds contributors, not the maintainer, so a direct push to `main` by the owner
# bypasses this file entirely - no review, no required checks. That is recorded
# in docs/runbook.md 3 and 6 rather than hidden. It means this file protects the
# workflows from everyone except the one person who can also change this file.
#
# This rule, not a directory layout, is what makes those files owned. The
# workflows briefly lived in infra/ as well so that "the pipeline is infra's";
# that mirror bought nothing and cost a red pull request per action bump
# (infra/README.md). Ownership belongs in this file, not in a path.
#
# The `*` line already assigns every path to the same owner, so the four
# specific rules below change no outcome while there is a single maintainer.
# They are kept because they are the list of paths whose ownership must survive
# a second maintainer being added: the day `*` becomes a team, these four must
# not silently widen with it.

